WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Random numbers are often quiet security boundaries.

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:10.250
They may appear in session identifiers, password reset links, CSRF tokens, API tokens, cryptographic keys, nonces, initialization vectors, salts,

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.615
challenges, correlation identifiers, and temporary names.

4
00:00:12.615 --> 00:00:22.077
In many systems, a random-looking value is what prevents one user from guessing another user's resource, reusing a sensitive workflow, or confusing

5
00:00:22.077 --> 00:00:24.048
one security event with another.

6
00:00:24.048 --> 00:00:29.568
When that value needs to be unpredictable, weak randomness can undercut otherwise careful design.

7
00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:32.327
Not every random value needs cryptographic strength.

8
00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:40.212
A randomized test input, game behavior, sampling decision, or load distribution choice may value repeatability, fairness, or statistical shape more

9
00:00:40.212 --> 00:00:41.000
than secrecy.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:49.279
Security-sensitive values have a different requirement: they must remain difficult to predict even when an observer understands the software design

11
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:51.645
and may have seen previous outputs.

12
00:00:51.645 --> 00:00:58.347
That distinction should be made before choosing an API, not after code has spread through the project.

13
00:00:58.347 --> 00:01:03.077
Defensive C++ engineering treats random-number generation as part of design review.

14
00:01:03.077 --> 00:01:11.356
Teams should identify which values are security-sensitive, choose approved CSPRNG sources for those values, avoid predictable seeds, and make unsafe

15
00:01:11.356 --> 00:01:12.145
fallbacks unacceptable.

16
00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:16.481
The goal is not to turn every developer into a cryptographer.

17
00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:25.155
The goal is to create clear defaults, safe wrappers, reviewable call sites, and operational rules that keep security randomness from becoming a

18
00:01:25.155 --> 00:01:27.520
casual utility call with hidden consequences.
